  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1255 PM CST WED NOV 11 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery shows several disturbances moving through the\narea today. A southern stream shortwave/speed max is producing an\narea of virga and very light rain across our southern counties.\nWhile most METARs are only reporting a trace of precipitation, a\ncouple spots have recorded 0.01\". Therefore, some adjustments to\nPoPs were made, and highs were also lowered in the southeast\ncounties. Additional weak disturbances are causing a few\nsprinkles and very light rain showers in our northern counties,\nwhile a stronger northern stream shortwave is moving through the\nMidwest. The latter feature is associated with a strong cold\nfront currently moving into the Ozarks. The HRRR and REFS members\nindicate some scattered shower development along the front as it\nmoves into the area this evening. Forecast soundings indicate\nmeager low-level moisture with dew points in the 50s, and a warm\nlayer aloft, which should inhibit too much vertical development.\nREFS thunder probabilities are 10 percent or less, so will not\ninclude any thunder mention in the official forecast.\n\nA deep trough will amplify across the eastern CONUS through\nSaturday, with strong cold air advection across Central Alabama.\nA secondary cold front associated with another vort max moving\nthrough the trough will move into the area Thursday night. This\ncould squeeze a couple more very light showers or sprinkles,\nwhich will need to be monitored for future PoP  updates. Winds\nwill stay up a bit behind the front Thursday night. This should\nkeep low temperatures in our far NW counties just above freezing,\nand could be a limiting factor for frost formation. Will hold off\non any frost/freeze products for Thursday night for now. High\ntemperatures Friday and Saturday will be similar to wintertime\nnormals in the 50s. Chances for the first freeze of the season\nremain high for Friday night and Saturday night, with the extent\nand magnitude dependent on winds/decoupling. This should end the\ngrowing season for some of the northern counties at minimum.\n\nA southern stream shortwave trough will undercut the amplified\nridge over the central CONUS over the weekend, and eventually\nmove through the area Monday. This will induce a weak wave of low\npressure to our south. This may result in some cold rain for\nparts of the area, but PoPs remain in the 20-30% range with\nuncertainty in the northward extent of any rain.\n\n32/JDavis",
